GER, AND MICHAEL J. BAIME\n                                        University of Pennsylvania, Philadelphia, Pennsylvania\n\n             Mindfulness is defined as paying attention in the present moment. We investigate the hypothesis that mindful-\n          ness training may alter or enhance specific aspects of attention. We examined three functionally and neuroana-\n          tomically distinct but overlapping attentional subsystems: alerting, orienting, and conflict monitoring. Func-\n          tioning of each subsystem was indexed by performance on the Attention Network Test (ANT; Fan, McCandliss,\n          Sommer, Raz, & Posner, 2002). Two types of mindfulness training (MT) programs were examined, and behav-\n          ioral testing was conducted on participants before (Time 1) and after (Time 2) training. One training group\n          consisted of individuals naive to mindfulness techniques who participated in an 8-week mindfulness-based\n          stress reduction (MBSR) course that emphasized the development of concentrative meditation skills. The other\n          training group consisted of individuals experienced in concentrative meditation techniques who participated\n          in a 1-month intensive mindfulness retreat. Performance of these groups was compared with that of control\n          participants who were meditation naive and received no MT. At Time 1, the participants in the retreat group\n          demonstrated improved conflict monitoring performance relative to those in the MBSR and control groups. At\n          Time 2, the participants in the MBSR course demonstrated significantly improved orienting in comparison with\n          the control and retreat participants. In contrast, the participants in the retreat group demonstrated altered perfor-\n          mance on the alerting component, with improvements in exogenous stimulus detection in comparison with the\n          control and MBSR participants. The groups did not differ in conflict monitoring performance at Time 2. These\n          results suggest that mindfulness training may improve attention-related behavioral responses by enhancing func-\n          tioning of specific subcomponents of attention. Whereas participation in the MBSR course improved the ability\n          to endogenously orient attention, retreat participation appeared to allow for the development and emergence of\n          receptive attentional skills, which improved exogenous alerting-related process.\n\n\n\n   Mindfulness has been defined as a process of “bringing             techniques (Kabat-Zinn, 2003; Kabat-Zinn et al., 1992).\none’s complete attention to the present experience on a               The effectiveness of MBSR for a variety of physical and\nmoment-to-moment basis” (Marlatt & Kristeller, 1999,                  psychological disorders has been examined in many stud-\np. 68) and as “paying attention in a particular way, on               ies (see Grossman, Niemann, Schmidt, & Walach, 2004).\npurpose, in the present moment, and nonjudgmentally”                  There is growing evidence that MBSR is effective in the\n(Kabat-Zinn, 1994, p. 4). Numerous meditation-based                   treatment of many conditions, including anxiety disorders,\npractices are used to train individuals to experience                 depression, fibromyalgia, chronic pain, substance abuse,\nmindfulness. The most widely practiced form of mind-                  binge eating, and skin diseases (Astin, 1997; Kabat-Zinn,\nfulness training (MT) is known as sitting meditation. In              1990; Kabat-Zinn, Lipworth, & Burney, 1985; Kabat-\nthis technique, participants are instructed to sit in a re-           Zinn et al., 1992; Kristeller & Hallett, 1999; Marlatt &\nlaxed, upright posture and to direct their full attention to          Kristeller, 1999; Speca, Carlson, Goodey, & Angen, 2000;\nthe sensations of breathing. They are instructed to return            Teasdale et al., 2000). In addition to MBSR programs,\ntheir attention to the breath whenever it wanders. Thus, a            several other settings offer MT, including meditation\nfundamental aspect of MT is attentional training, and the             centers and intensive retreat settings where mindfulness\ntask instructions of mindfulness techniques emphasize the             techniques are practiced for 10 h or more daily (Forte,\nrole of attention.                                                    Brown, & Dysart, 1987–1988). Several studies report that\n   Although MT has its roots in numerous cultural medita-             MT in these contexts also results in health improvements\ntion practices (Wallace, 1999), it has recently become more           (Brown, Forte, & Dysart, 1984; Forte et al., 1987–1988;\nwidely available in medical contexts. Over 250 medical                Page et al., 1997; Tori, 1999).\ncenters around the United States offer mindfulness-based                 Thus, MT is finding increasing use as a tool to treat\nstress reduction (MBSR) programs. These programs are                  numerous psychological and physical maladies and to en-\ntypically conducted as courses that meet once a week for              hance health and quality of life (Grossman et al., 2004).\n8 weeks and teach participants to use mindfulness-based               Yet, surprisingly little is known about how MT affects the\n\n\n                                                   A. P. Jha, apjha@psych.upenn.edu\n\n\n                                                                  109                  Copyright 2007 Psychonomic Society, Inc.\n\n110      JHA, KROMPINGER, AND BAIME\n\nneural mechanisms that regulate cognition and emotion.               There are striking parallels between the concentrative/re-\nRecent conceptualizations suggest that MT improves the            ceptive dichotomy described in many meditation texts and\nself-regulation of attention (see Bishop et al., 2004). Al-       recent cogni