{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"set -euo pipefail\nART=\\\"/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/mind/sources/artifacts/source-02-mindfulness-training-modifies-subsystems-of-attention-w2132002500--696402dc8f78/source.md\\\"\nprintf '%s\\\\n' '--- significant/contrast snippets ---'\nrg -n -C 2 \\\"significant|MBSR|retreat|orienting|conflict|alerting|correlation|difference scores|Figure 2|Figure 3|Figure 4\\\" \\\"\"'$ART\" | sed -n '\"'1,260p'\"","aggregated_output":"--- significant/contrast snippets ---\n13-             Mindfulness is defined as paying attention in the present moment. We investigate the hypothesis that mindful-\n14-          ness training may alter or enhance specific aspects of attention. We examined three functionally and neuroana-\n15:          tomically distinct but overlapping attentional subsystems: alerting, orienting, and conflict monitoring. Func-\n16-          tioning of each subsystem was indexed by performance on the Attention Network Test (ANT; Fan, McCandliss,\n17-          Sommer, Raz, & Posner, 2002). Two types of mindfulness training (MT) programs were examined, and behav-\n18-          ioral testing was conducted on participants before (Time 1) and after (Time 2) training. One training group\n19-          consisted of individuals naive to mindfulness techniques who participated in an 8-week mindfulness-based\n20:          stress reduction (MBSR) course that emphasized the development of concentrative meditation skills. The other\n21-          training group consisted of individuals experienced in concentrative meditation techniques who participated\n22:          in a 1-month intensive mindfulness retreat. Performance of these groups was compared with that of control\n23:          participants who were meditation naive and received no MT. At Time 1, the participants in the retreat group\n24:          demonstrated improved conflict monitoring performance relative to those in the MBSR and control groups. At\n25:          Time 2, the participants in the MBSR course demonstrated significantly improved orienting in comparison with\n26:          the control and retreat participants. In contrast, the participants in the retreat group demonstrated altered perfor-\n27:          mance on the alerting component, with improvements in exogenous stimulus detection in comparison with the\n28:          control and MBSR participants. The groups did not differ in conflict monitoring performance at Time 2. These\n29-          results suggest that mindfulness training may improve attention-related behavioral responses by enhancing func-\n30:          tioning of specific subcomponents of attention. Whereas participation in the MBSR course improved the ability\n31:          to endogenously orient attention, retreat participation appeared to allow for the development and emergence of\n32:          receptive attentional skills, which improved exogenous alerting-related process.\n33-\n34-\n35-\n36-   Mindfulness has been defined as a process of “bringing             techniques (Kabat-Zinn, 2003; Kabat-Zinn et al., 1992).\n37:one’s complete attention to the present experience on a               The effectiveness of MBSR for a variety of physical and\n38-moment-to-moment basis” (Marlatt & Kristeller, 1999,                  psychological disorders has been examined in many stud-\n39-p. 68) and as “paying attention in a particular way, on               ies (see Grossman, Niemann, Schmidt, & Walach, 2004).\n40:purpose, in the present moment, and nonjudgmentally”                  There is growing evidence that MBSR is effective in the\n41-(Kabat-Zinn, 1994, p. 4). Numerous meditation-based                   treatment of many conditions, including anxiety disorders,\n42-practices are used to train individuals to experience                 depression, fibromyalgia, chronic pain, substance abuse,\n--\n45-this technique, participants are i