{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'set -euo pipefail\nOUT=\"/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/mind/runs/run-20260426T075243Z--34k57xhutd/semantic/ingest_extraction.json\"\nmkdir -p \"$(dirname \"$OUT\")\"\ncat > \"$OUT\" <<'\"'JSON'\n{\n  \\\"source_date_inference\\\": {\n    \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"unknown\\\",\n    \\\"date\\\": null,\n    \\\"origin\\\": \\\"unknown\\\",\n    \\\"rationale\\\": null\n  },\n  \\\"source\\\": {\n    \\\"display_title\\\": \\\"Mindfulness Training Modifies Subsystems of Attention\\\",\n    \\\"authors\\\": [\n      \\\"Amishi P. Jha\\\",\n      \\\"Jason Krompinger\\\",\n      \\\"Michael J. Baime\\\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \\\"claims\\\": [\n    {\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"Mindfulness training can improve attention-related behavioral responses by changing specific subcomponents of attention rather than producing one uniform attentional benefit.\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"Training modifies attention subsystems\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\n        \\\"The study examined three functionally and neuroanatomically distinct but overlapping attentional subsystems--alerting, orienting, and conflict monitoring--using the Attention Network Test before and after mindfulness training.\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"Participants with prior concentrative meditation experience showed reduced conflict-monitoring interference at baseline compared with meditation-naive participants.\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"Experience reduces conflict interference\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\n        \\\"At Time 1, the collapsed MBSR and control groups differed from the retreat group on conflict monitoring for response time, t(1,49)=2.22, p=.03, and accuracy, t(1,49)=5.62, p<.001; the conflict-monitoring effect was reduced in the retreat group.\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"An 8-week MBSR course improved endogenous orienting performance at Time 2 relative to both controls and retreat participants.\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"MBSR improves endogenous orienting\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\n        \\\"At Time 2, orienting response-time difference scores showed a significant group effect, F(2,48)=10.13, p<.001; the MBSR group differed from controls after Bonferroni adjustment, p=.046, and from the retreat group, p<.001.\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"After retreat training, experienced meditators detected targets more efficiently when no warning cue was provided, consistent with enhanced exogenous alerting readiness.\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"Retreat improves no-cue detection\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\n        \\\"At Time 2, alerting response-time scores differed between the retreat group and the collapsed control plus MBSR group, t(1,49)=3.55, p<.001; younger retreat participants had significantly shorter no-cue response times than the comparison group, t(1,40)=2.17, p=.03.\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"Conflict-monitoring differences were not observed across groups at Time 2, plausibly because task exposure improved performance across all groups and masked training-specific gains.\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"Conflict gains equalize over time\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\n        \\\"At Time 2, the conflict-monitoring response-time and accuracy difference scores had no significant main effects and no significant paired contrasts, p>.36; the authors report that control participants improved in conflict monitoring from Time 1 to Time 2 without attention training.\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"More months of prior meditation experience in the retreat group predicted smaller alerting scores at Time 2.\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"Experience reduces alerting scores\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\n        \\\"For retreat participants, months of meditation experience correlated with Time 2 alerting score, R=-.52, t=2.4, p=.03, indicating a significant reduction in alerting score with increased meditation experience.\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"The study interprets concentrative practice as primarily affecting dorsal attention functions and receptive practice as primarily affecting ventral alerting functions.\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"Practice maps attention systems\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\n        \\\"The discussion links conflict monitoring and orienting to voluntary attentional selection in the dorsal attention system, while receptive attention is described as corresponding to ventral attention functions of exogenous stimulus detection, attentional readiness, and alerting.\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"The reported findings are preliminary because the training groups were small, the controls did not receive an active alternative training program, and stress-reduction mechanisms were not separated from attention-training mechanisms.\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"Small samples limit inference\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\n        \\\"The discussion states that the results should be interpreted cautiously because the experiment was among the first to evaluate mindfulness training and attentional subsystems, training groups were relatively small, and future work should include active-training controls, neural measures, and better characterization of training protocols.\\\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \\\"evidence_items\\\": [\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-ant-three-subsystems-design--e5f6a7b8c9d0\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"ANT Subsystem Design\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"Participants in MBSR, retreat, and control groups performed the Attention Network Test at Time 1 and Time 2. The task used four cue conditions--no cue, double cue, center cue, and spatial cue--and two target conditions--congruent and incongruent flankers--with response time and accuracy difference scores used to index alerting, orienting, and conflict monitoring.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"This design is the operational backbone of the paper. It connects mindfulness-training hypotheses to separable behavioral markers rather than relying on self-report, allowing the authors to ask whether training changes alerting, orienting, and conflict monitoring differently. The design supports broad claims about subsystem-specific effects, while its behavioral-only nature limits direct neural conclusions.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"model_constraint\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"0\\\",\n        \\\"6\\\"\n      ],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-02-mindfulness-training-modifies-subsystems-of-attention-w2132002500--696402dc8f78\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"pp.109-113\\\",\n        \\\"Figure 1\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-training-groups-and-doses--f6a7b8c9d0e1\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Training Groups and Doses\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"The MBSR group included 17 meditation-naive participants in an 8-week course meeting once weekly for 3 hours with assigned 30-minute daily sitting practice. The retreat group entered a 1-month residential retreat with prior concentrative meditation experience ranging from 4 to 360 months and practiced formal mindfulness meditation for 10-12 hours per day; the control group included 17 participants with no meditation experience.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"The group structure explains why the source distinguishes novice concentrative training from intensive retreat practice in experienced meditators. It also creates interpretive limits: training dose, prior experience, age range, and instructional context differ between groups, so observed performance differences are informative but not a clean randomized dose comparison.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"measurement\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"0\\\",\n        \\\"6\\\",\n        \\\"7\\\"\n      ],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-02-mindfulness-training-modifies-subsystems-of-attention-w2132002500--696402dc8f78\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"pp.112-113\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-conflict-monitoring-retreat-baseline--a1b2c3d4e5f6\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Baseline Conflict Reduction\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"At Time 1, MBSR and control participants did not significantly differ, so their scores were collapsed and compared with the retreat group. Conflict monitoring showed significant group differences for response time, t(1,49)=2.22, p=.03, and accuracy, t(1,49)=5.62, p<.001; the magnitude of conflict monitoring was reduced in retreat participants.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"This statistical result grounds the claim that prior concentrative meditation experience was associated with better baseline conflict monitoring. Because the retreat group already had meditation experience before Time 1, the finding supports an experience-linked association rather than proving that the current retreat caused the baseline advantage.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"statistical_result\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"1\\\",\n        \\\"6\\\"\n      ],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-02-mindfulness-training-modifies-subsystems-of-attention-w2132002500--696402dc8f78\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"p.114\\\",\n        \\\"Figure 2\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-mbsr-orienting-time-two--b2c3d4e5f6a7\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"MBSR Orienting Improvement\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"At Time 2, an ANOVA on orienting response-time difference scores revealed a significant group factor, F(2,48)=10.13, p<.001. Planned contrasts showed that the MBSR group differed from the control group, Bonferroni-adjusted p=.046, and from the retreat group, Bonferroni-adjusted p<.001; retreat and control groups did not differ, p=.161.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"This is the central evidence for a selective MBSR effect on endogenous orienting. The result indicates that the novice training program emphasizing repeated attentional engagement was linked to a larger orienting benefit, while the absence of an accuracy effect and the near-significant spatial-cue comparison keep the interpretation focused on response-time efficiency.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"statistical_result\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"2\\\",\n        \\\"6\\\"\n      ],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-02-mindfulness-training-modifies-subsystems-of-attention-w2132002500--696402dc8f78\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"pp.114-115\\\",\n        \\\"Figure 3\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-retreat-alerting-time-two--c3d4e5f6a7b8\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Retreat Alerting Shift\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"At Time 2, alerting response-time scores significantly differed between the retreat group and the collapsed control plus MBSR group, t(1,49)=3.55, p<.001, while accuracy did not differ, p>.7. In the younger retreat comparison, no-cue response times were shorter than controls plus MBSR, t(1,40)=2.17, p=.03, but double-cue response times were not, p>.6.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"This result grounds the paper's receptive-attention interpretation. The reduced alerting score is not treated as reduced alertness; it reflects better target detection when no temporal or spatial warning cue is available. Because the explicit no-cue comparison uses the younger retreat subgroup, age remains an important scope condition.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"statistical_result\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"3\\\",\n        \\\"6\\\"\n      ],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-02-mindfulness-training-modifies-subsystems-of-attention-w2132002500--696402dc8f78\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"p.115\\\",\n        \\\"Figure 4\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-meditation-experience-alerting-correlation--d4e5f6a7b8c9\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Experience Predicts Alerting\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"For retreat participants, months of meditation experience before the retreat were correlated with the magnitude of the Time 2 alerting score. The reported correlation was R=-.52 and was significant, t=2.4, p=.03, showing a significant reduction in alerting score with increased meditation experience.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"The correlation supports the idea that retreat-related alerting changes depended on prior meditation history rather than retreat attendance alone. It strengthens the paper's trajectory account in which receptive attention emerges after concentrative experience, but it remains correlational and confined to the retreat sample.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"statistical_result\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"5\\\",\n        \\\"6\\\"\n      ],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-02-mindfulness-training-modifies-subsystems-of-attention-w2132002500--696402dc8f78\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"p.115\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-time-two-conflict-null-result--0a1b2c3d4e5f\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Conflict Null at Time Two\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"At Time 2, the conflict-monitoring response-time and accuracy difference scores showed no significant main effects and no significant effects in paired contrasts, p>.36. A separate time analysis found an overall response-time speeding effect, F(1,16)=13.6, p<.001, without group interactions.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"This evidence prevents overgeneralizing the mindfulness-training effect to every attention subsystem. It also motivates the authors' caution that repeated exposure to the ANT may have improved conflict-monitoring performance across all groups, potentially masking training-specific changes at Time 2.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"statistical_result\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"4\\\",\n        \\\"7\\\"\n      ],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-02-mindfulness-training-modifies-subsystems-of-attention-w2132002500--696402dc8f78\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"pp.114-115\\\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \\\"relations\\\": [],\n  \\\"summary\\\": \\\"Jha, Krompinger, and Baime report that mindfulness training changes attention in subsystem-specific ways. Using the Attention Network Test, they found baseline conflict-monitoring advantages in experienced meditators, Time 2 orienting gains after MBSR, and Time 2 alerting changes after intensive retreat practice. The interpretation is cautious because training dose, prior experience, age, passive controls, and task-repetition effects limit causal precision.\\\",\n  \\\"warnings\\\": [\n    {\n      \\\"code\\\": \\\"missing_publication_date\\\",\n      \\\"message\\\": \\\"Publication date could not be resolved from the source record; source_date_inference.date is null.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"code\\\": \\\"source_record_title_generic\\\",\n      \\\"message\\\": \\\"The source record title was generic, so display_title was recovered from the article heading in source.md.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"code\\\": \\\"pdf_text_math_glyphs_normalized\\\",\n      \\\"message\\\": \\\"The pdftotext extraction used degraded glyphs for equality and inequality symbols; statistical excerpts normalize those symbols conservatively from surrounding context.\\\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \\\"source_dossier\\\": {\n    \\\"summary_short\\\": \\\"This article tests whether mindfulness training changes distinct attentional subsystems rather than attention in general. MBSR, intensive retreat practice, and no-training control groups completed the Attention Network Test before and after the training interval. The main pattern links novice MBSR training with improved orienting and experienced retreat practice with altered alerting readiness, while conflict-monitoring effects were strongest at baseline in experienced meditators.\\\",\n    \\\"summary_long\\\": \\\"Jha, Krompinger, and Baime investigate mindfulness training as a behavioral attention-training protocol by separating attention into alerting, orienting, and conflict-monitoring components. Instead of relying on introspective reports, they use the Attention Network Test to obtain response-time and accuracy difference scores before and after training. The design compares three groups: meditation-naive participants in an 8-week MBSR course, experienced meditators in a 1-month intensive retreat, and meditation-naive controls who received no mindfulness training. The strongest claim is not that mindfulness improves all attention uniformly, but that different training contexts align with different attentional operations. At baseline, the retreat group already showed less conflict-monitoring interference than the meditation-naive participants, consistent with a link between prior concentrative practice and response-level selection. At Time 2, MBSR participants showed stronger orienting response-time benefits than controls and retreat participants, supporting the idea that repeated practice of engaging and returning attention can improve endogenous orienting. The retreat group, by contrast, showed reduced alerting scores and faster no-cue target detection, which the authors interpret as evidence for a more readied or receptive attentional state rather than reduced alertness. A correlation between prior meditation experience and smaller Time 2 alerting scores strengthens this interpretation. The paper is careful about limits: the retreat group differed in age range and prior experience, the control group was passive rather than active, stress reduction was not separated from attentional training, and repeated task exposure may have affected conflict monitoring. Overall, the source presents preliminary but concrete behavioral evidence that mindfulness practices may tune specific attentional subsystems, with concentrative and receptive modes mapping onto different functions.\\\",\n    \\\"sections\\\": [\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Research Question and Design\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The study asks whether mindfulness training changes particular attentional operations instead of producing a vague global improvement. It operationalizes attention through the Attention Network Test, using cue and flanker conditions to index alerting, orienting, and conflict monitoring across MBSR, retreat, and control groups tested before and after training.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\n          \\\"0\\\",\n          \\\"6\\\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Baseline Meditation Experience\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The clearest pre-training difference appears in conflict monitoring. Experienced retreat participants had smaller flanker-interference effects than the combined meditation-naive groups at Time 1, in both response time and accuracy. This supports an association between prior concentrative practice and better response-level selection, but it is not evidence that the upcoming retreat caused the baseline advantage.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\n          \\\"1\\\",\n          \\\"6\\\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"MBSR and Orienting\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"After the 8-week MBSR course, participants showed stronger orienting response-time difference scores than both controls and retreat participants. The authors interpret this as an improvement in endogenous orienting, plausibly tied to repeated practice of engaging attention, moving it back to the breath or body, and sustaining a selected focus.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\n          \\\"2\\\",\n          \\\"6\\\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Retreat and Alerting\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The retreat pattern differs from the MBSR pattern. Alerting scores were reduced after retreat, and the difference was driven by faster no-cue target detection rather than double-cue trials. The authors read this as a more readied, receptive attentional state: participants could detect stimuli efficiently even without warning information about when or where targets would appear.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\n          \\\"3\\\",\n          \\\"5\\\",\n          \\\"6\\\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Null Conflict Result\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The lack of Time 2 group differences in conflict monitoring is important because it blocks a simple all-purpose improvement story. The authors suggest that repeated exposure to the ANT may have improved conflict performance even in controls, making it harder to detect any extra training-related benefit and possibly creating floor effects in response time.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\n          \\\"4\\\",\n          \\\"7\\\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Assumptions and Limits\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The interpretation depends on mapping behavioral ANT components to dorsal and ventral attention systems and on distinguishing concentrative from receptive practice. Those mappings are plausible but indirect here because the study does not include neural measurements. Group differences in prior experience, training dose, age range, and motivation also constrain causal claims.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\n          \\\"6\\\",\n          \\\"7\\\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Practical Interpretation\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"For readers, the practical lesson is that mindfulness training should not be treated as one uniform cognitive intervention. Structured novice MBSR may be most relevant to deliberate orienting and selected focus, while intensive practice in experienced meditators may cultivate readiness to detect unanticipated events. The source is best read as an early, targeted behavioral demonstration rather than a final clinical prescription.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\n          \\\"0\\\",\n          \\\"2\\\",\n          \\\"3\\\",\n          \\\"7\\\"\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n}\nJSON\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
